# Chapter 10 — Animation

You can move a thing with velocity, and you can move it with a shove. Both are
*physics* — you set a force and the tick works it out. But a lot of the motion in
a game is not physics at all. A card that slides onto the screen, a coin that
bounces when you grab it, a title that fades in — those are *choreographed*. You
know exactly where they start, where they end, and how long the trip should take,
and you want the console to fill in every frame in between.

That filling-in has a name: a **tween** (from *in-between*). You give it a start,
an end, and a duration; the console tweens — it computes all the middle frames so
the motion looks smooth. This chapter is the animator: how to start a tween, ask
how many are running, and clear them all.

## At rest, nothing is animating

The console keeps a list of every tween currently in flight. `animation/tweens`
hands it to you, and at the start it is empty — you can count it with `length`:

```motoi
(display (length (animation/tweens)))
(newline)
```

```
0
```

Zero tweens. Nothing is being animated, so the list is empty and its length is
`0`. This count is your window on the animator: any time you want to know how
much motion the console is choreographing, you ask how long this list is.

## Bounce something

The friendliest animation verb is `bounce` — it lifts a target up by some height
and drops it back, the classic *I got picked up!* wiggle. Give it a thing to
bounce and a height, and it registers a tween:

```motoi
(bounce \"star\" :height 20)
(display (length (animation/tweens)))
(newline)
```

```
1
```

You bounced the text `\"star\"` up `20` pixels. `bounce` did not draw anything at
the prompt — it *registered* a tween, and now the animator's list has `1` thing
in it. On a live console that tween is already playing: the star rises and falls
over the next handful of frames. Here, we prove it started by counting the list.
The target can be a text string like this, or a sprite by name from Chapter 4.

## Ask how many are still moving

There is a second question you can ask the animator: not *how many exist*, but
*how many are still moving*. `animation/tick` advances every tween one step and
hands back the count of those not yet finished:

```motoi
(bounce \"star\" :height 20)
(display (animation/tick))
(newline)
```

```
1
```

One tween, and it is still in motion, so `animation/tick` reports `1`. In a
running console this verb fires once per frame — sixty times a second — and each
call nudges every tween a little further along its arc, returning the number
still going. When a tween reaches its end it drops out of the count. Watch that
number fall to `0` and you know every animation has finished.

## Run more than one at once

Animations stack. A real screen has several going at the same moment — a card
sliding in while a coin bounces while a label fades. Start a second one with
`slide` (which brings a thing in from an edge) and the list grows:

```motoi
(bounce \"star\" :height 20)
(slide \"card\" :from-side 'left)
(display (length (animation/tweens)))
(newline)
```

```
2
```

Two tweens now — the bounce and the slide — running side by side, each keeping
its own start, end, and timing. The animator carries as many as you start, and
`animation/tick` advances all of them together on every frame. This is how a
lively screen is built: not one motion at a time, but a whole handful,
choreographed at once.

## Clear the stage

When a scene ends — a menu closes, a level resets — you want the animator empty
and ready. `animation/clear!` drops every tween at once:

```motoi
(bounce \"star\" :height 20)
(slide \"card\" :from-side 'left)
(animation/clear!)
(display (length (animation/tweens)))
(newline)
```

```
0
```

Two tweens started, then `animation/clear!` wiped them, and the list is back to
`0`. This is the reset button for motion: no matter how many animations are in
flight, one call empties the stage. Reach for it whenever you switch scenes and
do not want yesterday's motion bleeding into today's.

## What you just proved

You met the animator. You counted an empty tween list (`0`), bounced a thing and
watched the list grow to `1`, asked `animation/tick` how many were still moving,
stacked a second animation to reach `2`, and cleared the whole stage back to `0`.
Every program ran and reported a real count you could check against the verbs you
called.

Tweens are *choreographed* motion — you say where and how long, and the console
fills the middle. Physics motion from Chapter 7 is the other half. Together they
cover almost everything that moves in a game. What is missing is what happens
when two moving things *touch* — the hero and the wall, the coin and the hand.
That meeting is called collision, and it is the next chapter.

> **Tip**
>
> Use tweens for motion you fully script — a slide-in, a bounce, a fade — and use
> velocity or `entity/accel!` from Chapter 7 for motion that emerges from forces.
> Mixing them up is a common mistake: a bouncing coin does not need gravity, and
> a falling player does not need a tween. Ask *do I know exactly where this ends?*
> — if yes, tween it; if no, let physics carry it.

> **Warning**
>
> `bounce`, `slide`, and `fade` register a tween; they do not finish it at the
> prompt. A tween completes only as frames advance, and nothing pumps frames at
> the bare prompt — so the count stays put here instead of falling to `0` on its
> own. This is not a stuck animation; it is an un-pumped one. In a live console
> the frame loop advances sixty times a second and every tween slides to its end.
> Appendix C covers the framebuffer and the animation layer in full.

